"""Install, remove and look up software from the portlin catalog.

This is the one program on the stick that installs things as root. The
Software app in the menu never touches apt: it runs this, through pkexec for
the catalog kinds that need root and directly for the ones that must not be
root, and reads the lines this prints. That keeps the surface that can be
asked to act as root a single command with a fixed set of verbs, rather
than a window.

The privilege rule cuts both ways. Kinds that write under /usr, /etc or /opt
refuse to run without root; kinds that run a vendor's installer into a home
directory refuse to run as root, because `sudo portlin-install install zed`
would otherwise scatter root-owned files through someone's home.

What it prints is meant for two readers at once. Lines beginning with `::`
are for the app: a step that began, a percentage, a warning, a request to
reboot, and the result. Everything else is apt, curl and dpkg talking, passed
through unchanged so a person in a terminal sees what they would have seen
running the commands by hand.

    ::step <text>            a phase began
    ::progress <0-100>       how far through the current step apt is
    ::warn <text>            worth showing, not fatal
    ::reboot                 a kernel module was built; reboot before relying on it
    ::result ok|failed <id> [text]

Every install is planned first, as a list of steps, and only then run. The
planners are pure functions of the catalog entry and a description of the
system, which is what lets the exact commands for every entry be asserted by
unit tests on a machine with no apt at all.
"""

